4. If you have installed Node.js, run the following commands from the root /NYU_SWE project directory to install all required packages for the frontend React.js server.

```powershell
$ cd frontend

$ npm install
```

5. Once JavaScript package installation is complete, start the frontend server using this command in the /NYU_SWE/frontend directory.

```powershell
$ npm run start
```

6. The React development server will start running at [http://localhost:3000](http://localhost:3000).

## For Developers

### Creating an admin

To create an admin account for approving asset listing from users, run the folloring command in the /NYU_SWE/marketproj directory. A new admin user will be created with the username **admin** and password **admin**.

```powershell
$ python manage.py create_admin
```

### Formatting Standards

Coding standards are enforced for both frontend JavaScript (ESLint + Prettier) and backend Python code (flake8 + black).

1. To format all frontend files, run the following code from the root /NYU_SWE directory.

```powershell
$ cd frontend

$ npx prettier --write .

$ npx eslint . --fix
```

2. To format all backend files, run the following code from the root /NYU_SWE directory.

```powershell
$ cd marketproj

$ black .
```

Eventually, formatting will be automatic upon commit and/or push.

### Full Deployment Update Workflow with Docker

**_Ensure that Docker is installed locally before attempting to modify a deployment!!!_**

If you want to run Docker containers locally, use the following command in the root /NYU_SWE directory.

```powershell
$ docker-compose up -d
```

**_CAREFUL_**: Whenever you update code of the cloud deployment:

1. Rebuild locally

```powershell
$ cd frontend

$ docker build -t likhith2366/divy:frontend .
```

```powershell
$ cd marketproj

$ docker build -t likhith2366/divy:backend .
```

2. Push to Docker Hub

```powershell
$ docker push likhith2366/divy:frontend

$ docker push likhith2366/divy:backend
```

3. SSH into EC2 & update

```powershell
$ sude docker-compose pull
$ sude docker-compose up -d
```

The deployment will be instantly refreshed.

